#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "0fc616fa-c901-53ff-acab-cdada6bb5ce2" :question/math-academy-id "e-80" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{3x^{4} + 9x}{6x^{4} - x^{3}} &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{3x^{4}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{9x}{x^{4}})}{(\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{4}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(3 + \\frac{9}{x^{3}})}{(6 - \\frac{1}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b648ed80-e2e1-57c4-bd37-147d92315e90" :question/math-academy-id "e-81" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{4 - 5x - 2x^{3}}{x^{2} + 3} &= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{-2x^{3}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{5x}{x^{2}} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(-2x - \\frac{5}{x} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(\\frac{-2x + 0 + 0}{1 + 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(-2x) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c3798fe4-058f-5970-a903-65c654abb788" :question/math-academy-id "e-82" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{2x^{3} + 1}{x^{3} - 15x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{15x}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(2 + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{15}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 0}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/id #uuid "32a8e4bb-4191-55a8-883b-baa36f67b061" :question/math-academy-id "e-83" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{x^{5} - 10}{x^{3} - 8x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{x^{5}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{8x^{2}}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(x^{2} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{8}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}(\\frac{x^{2} - 0}{1 - 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}x^{2} \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d289c4d-66f9-5bc3-ba56-6f40a3dd4628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275157} {:answer/id #uuid "57140c10-c243-59a6-a36d-d4ee1187cf49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275158} {:answer/id #uuid "eec3b875-66a9-5dff-878d-62bcc4f5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275159} {:answer/id #uuid "938099ab-6674-5275-bf78-41bf3fd9fe1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275160} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd6c2f5-fa61-51d4-8143-2a3ff0cb7e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec3b875-66a9-5dff-878d-62bcc4f5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275159} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf9256b-03e8-5ac3-88fd-10615dd8ed9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062f25d0-e65b-58a6-96b7-d2cc83f47308" :question/math-academy-id "q-106702" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{11{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11-9x)}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11-9x}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(11-9x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74885b07-6eb9-512f-88de-44f3f84c5a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74885b07-6eb9-512f-88de-44f3f84c5a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170} :answer-field/id #uuid "59067eab-dbc9-5efb-a806-44544690b377" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c34786e-7329-5ee5-938e-61e317be395c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339612" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} {:answer/id #uuid "1336ebc1-00e8-5060-99c4-9d61efe3b6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf039c6-d675-57a2-b9d8-6e8d13947e79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a616dfb2-527b-5098-b8ce-2796ed00b6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339623" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b092275-0441-51f6-a010-037dfba6f9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121985} {:answer/id #uuid "97fada48-c296-548c-b940-338cfffbd117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121986} {:answer/id #uuid "e2569ab0-02c7-5ac5-b846-3a3d0cca1aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121987} {:answer/id #uuid "01ab5ced-488e-53de-a635-3fb03079cdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988} {:answer/id #uuid "7f242d0b-0fd1-5380-b194-6c29749e0d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ab5ced-488e-53de-a635-3fb03079cdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b4b97e-25e9-5c1e-b9ae-450448f2ae46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24c1ddd3-5086-5a99-bc61-14c84e2545e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35763" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 5}{2x^{3} + 2x + 6}$." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc15ea9-a192-5001-abcb-6f10667addd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123053} {:answer/id #uuid "1304015b-bb22-5ba8-91cc-0f7de191cefc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123054} {:answer/id #uuid "4188dd24-9fe5-51f1-926d-ae98d672193b" :answer/type {:d